; ps2_keyboard_rx.mas -- PS/2 device-to-host receiver (blind-challenge program, item 6).
;
; Specification: IBM Personal System/2 Hardware Interface Technical Reference (1988), "Keyboard-to-System
; Communication" (scanned: https://archive.org/details/bitsavers_ibm); mirrored as "The PS/2 Mouse/Keyboard
; Protocol" (A. Chapweske, 1999), "Communication: Device-to-Host",
; https://www.burtonsys.com/ps2_chapweske.htm .
; The device owns both wires: it drives the clock (10-16.7 kHz) and data, both open drain with pull-ups.
; A frame is 11 clock falls: start (0), 8 data bits LSB first, odd parity, stop (1).  Data is valid on the
; falling clock edge.  The chip only listens; it never drives either wire.
; Tested in code/tests/vendor_models/ps2_test.py against ps2_keyboard_peer.v (first-party, written from the
; same specification); the Score version of the same protocol is code/tests/vendor_models/ps2_keyboard.score.
;
;   * Line 0 is a follower of P0 (CLK fall).  Tap 0 is edge-sourced on the same fall, so the capture in the
;     snapshot is the DATA level (P1) at that edge; `in0 a0` makes L.IN bit 0 that capture.
;   * Thread 0 (core 0, trigger L0.TAP0) runs one ten-instruction burst per clock fall:
;       - ACC counts bit positions 1..11 (1 = start, 2..9 = data, 10 = parity, 11 = stop); the wrap sets the
;         index back to 0 so the next start bit is 1 again;
;       - CNT0 is the data-bit counter: reloaded to 8 on the start bit (idx <= 1), so the shifter takes exactly
;         the eight data bits and freezes; SH0 (LSB first, 8 bits) assembles the byte;
;       - S1 logs the raw 11-bit frame so the host can check start, parity and stop itself;
;       - the assembled byte goes to HOST.OUT once CNT0 is 0 (parity bit onwards; the shifter is frozen, so the
;         parity and stop bits rewrite the same byte).
;   * Frame resynchronisation is by bit count since reset: PS/2 frames are back-to-back with no idle marker the
;     language can see, so a lost edge shifts the frame.  The host checks the raw log for that case.
;
; Pins: CLK = P0 (uio[0]), DATA = P1 (uio[1]).  No output pins are used.

.clock 60e6

.line 0
    follow
    period 40000            ; MOD = longest CLK period tolerated (667 us; PS/2 is 60-100 us)
    edge P0 fall            ; boundary: every falling clock edge
    t0edge P0 fall          ; tap 0: the same edge, for the data capture
    pins A=P1               ; DATA
    in0 a0
    snap tap0
    minperiod 3000          ; shortest CLK period tolerated (3000 cycles = 50 us, above 16.7 kHz)
    minhalf 1000
    ctl run ren
.end

.stream 1
    mode rx
    order lsb
    start 0x100
    len 0x100
.end

.thread 0
    core 0
    line 0
    trigger L0.TAP0
    start bit
.end

.budget thread 0 cycles 14

.core 0
bit:    #1 -> ACC.ADD
        #11 -> ACC.CMP
        when ACC.C   ? ZERO -> ACC.LD
        L.IN -> S1.RX
        when CNT0.Z   ! L.IN -> SH0.SH
        when CNT0.Z   ! ZERO -> CNT0.DEC
        #2 -> ACC.CMP
        when ACC.C   ! #8 -> CNT0.LO
        when CNT0.Z   ? SH0.D -> HOST.OUT
        pause goto bit
